#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Maps a branch's changed files to the tests that cover them, and prints the
commands to run before a push (owner, 2026-11-11).

Usage: plan/bin/affected-tests [BASE]          # BASE defaults to origin/master
       plan/bin/affected-tests --stdin < FILES  # one repo-relative path a line

It reads `git diff --name-only BASE...HEAD`, plus the uncommitted and
untracked files, and prints for each file the rule that maps it and its
targets, then:

- whether the diff needs Postgres locally: models, migrations, services,
  querysets or raw SQL, the database settings, or `core/tests/populated.py`.
  A module outside the tests that imports a models module or django.db,
  makes an ORM write call or runs raw SQL counts whatever lines changed;
  in any other file only the changed lines count. Otherwise CI's Postgres
  jobs cover it;
- which browser tests measure text, and so also run with
  `DERBYNET_BROWSER_FONT="DejaVu Sans"`, which draws `system-ui` as CI does
  (#273's "Heat 100" failed only there);
- the repeats under load each changed browser test file takes (D85): 25 for
  one that involves timing, 5 per font for a deterministic one, read from
  the whole file, of the tests its diff adds or changes, by node id (D101,
  amending D85 beside D97). Compared by AST at BASE and HEAD, a top-level
  test or a test class's test method repeats when it is new or changed, or
  when it reads, directly or through another helper or test of the file, a
  helper (an undecorated def that is no test and nothing pytest reads by
  its name) or a test the diff adds, changes or deletes; a test method also
  when it reads such a method of its class through `self`, `cls` or the
  class's name. A new test class repeats whole. The whole file repeats
  when anything else changes: a fixture, a hook (an xunit setup or
  teardown pytest calls by name too, `XUNIT`), pytestmark, an import or other
  module-level code that is no part of a D97 move, a test class outside its
  test methods; when a changed helper is read by nothing in the file, or by
  such code, or a changed test method is read by its class outside its test
  methods; when a name is bound twice, by `global` or by an assignment
  expression (:=) outside a function; when a docstring changes and the file
  reads `__doc__`; when the diff changes no test or helper by AST; and for
  a file new on the branch, one that can't be parsed, or `--stdin`. The
  module's docstring, a test class's, and comments are no change. None
  repeat for an import-only move (D97, amending D85): a diff that only
  changes imports and deletes top-level defs, classes or constants into a
  module M that has them unchanged, by AST. Each deleted name is imported
  by name (`from M import name`), or is a dependency the moved code took
  along into M: a name the moved code reads, which nothing the file keeps
  reads, and which is not decorated (nor, for a constant, built by a call),
  since pytest finds a fixture by its presence. Each name the moved code
  reads is bound alike in M, and one the file binds by a statement moved
  too. An import goes only with the moved code that alone read it. A
  changed body or docstring, a new test or comment, a copy that differs, an
  import that binds any other name anew or a name otherwise, an assignment
  expression (:=) outside a function, `from __future__` imports that
  differ, or what can't be parsed or resolved (a relative or star import, a
  module outside the tree) is no import-only move. A flake ticket keeps 25
  repeats of the whole file, on the database it failed on, whatever the
  tool prints (D101);
- before the commands, `tools/docstring_claims.py` on each changed test file,
  then with `--check` on the PR body draft over every one of them, since a
  catch-all row fails (#341 retro), with `--base BASE`, so that it asks only
  for the claims of the tests the diff adds or changes and of those that
  read a helper it adds or changes (D123; not under `--stdin`), and
  `tools/operand_mutants.py` when a changed hand-written source file can
  hold a condition (not a migration or vendored code), plan/'s Python
  tools and tests among them, with `tools/mutate.py --listed`, which runs
  every mutant it lists (#336 retro),
  and `--listed --file` on each changed test file for its helpers (#345
  retro): run them before the first hand-off run,
  since a claim or a mutant they find means an edit, and the run starts
  again (#312 retro);
- the commands, from server/.

A test file maps to itself and, like a test helper (a module in a tests
folder that is no test file or conftest), to each test file and conftest
folder that imports it, directly or through any other file of a tests
folder, a test file too (#371 retro: test_kiosk_banner.py imports
open_kiosk from test_kiosk_pages.py). When a helper's diff from BASE only
adds top-level functions, by AST, it maps instead to the importers that
name an added function as a whole word, and to every file that imports
one of them, with a conftest's folder (#371). An added
def has no decorator and only literal defaults; an added import beside it
counts too; no added name is one the module bound or read at BASE, or one
pytest or Python reads by its presence, a test's among them; the module
docstring may change unless the module reads `__doc__` (D101). Anything
else keeps every importer, and so do a star import among the importers, an
importer that can't be parsed, no importer that names an added function, a
new or deleted helper, and `--stdin`; the output says why.

A shared conftest, pyproject.toml, uv.lock, the settings or a CI workflow
reaches every suite on both databases (#282's browser conftest hook ran in
every collection), so CI's full run is the check, and the commands are the
targeted tests (D85). A file no rule maps falls back to its app's tests, and
the output says so; outside server/ it falls back to CI's full run. It never maps
a file to nothing without saying why (docs, the frozen PHP, a server tool
no test names or imports).

`--stdin` reads the file list instead of git, for a back-test
(`git diff --name-only SHA^ SHA | plan/bin/affected-tests --stdin`); it then
looks for database code in each whole file rather than in its diff, and
has no BASE to find an import-only move or a changed test against, so
every changed browser test file keeps its count, of the whole file.
`--root DIR` reads the tree at DIR instead of the repository's top level.
"""

import ast
import io
import re
import subprocess
import sys
import tokenize
from dataclasses import dataclass, field
from pathlib import Path

SERVER = 'server'

PAGE_WIDE = (  # plan/AGENT-BRIEF.md, UI pages
